#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199252} :activity/id #uuid "394fea4c-49c6-55ac-b019-2ee697e8297a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049032 :topic/id #uuid "d4ff589f-1af0-5477-b78d-91f6dfb7f322" :topic/math-academy-id 4134} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199252 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199452 :tutorial/content "The plane region $D$ shown below is defined as\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 1 \\leq xe^{y}\\leq 4, \\: \\: 1 \\leq x^2e^{-2y} \\leq 3 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/17/17e133a50f4a587a26fbf0914c1dc08106ecb822e1e88ba5e7280a10215221c0.png)\n\n \n\nThis region is pretty complicated, which could make it difficult to work with.\n\nSo, we'd like to define a transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf T^{-1}: (x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))\n$$\n\nthat maps the region $D$ to a rectangular region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane.  \n\n![](images/d2/d26df05b3200a01f2b6fba5a1c04e8d178c88f40e078309c71329bcdfcc91ac2.png)\n\n \n\nHow can we find such a transformation?\n\nLooking at the bounding curves that make up the structure of $D,$ we consider the transformation \n\n$$\n{\\color{red}u}={\\color{red}{xe^{y}}}, \\qquad {\\color{blue}v}={\\color{blue}{x^2e^{-2y}}}.\n$$\n\nNext, we find our expression for $\\Delta$ by writing $D$ in terms of $u$ and $v,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ={T}^{-1}(D) & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le x{e}^{y}\\le 4,\\,\\,1\\le {x}^{2}{e}^{-2y}\\le 3\\} \\\\  & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,\\,1\\le v\\le 3\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd that's all there is to it! We've successfully found a transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ that maps the complicated region $D$ in the $xy$-plane to the rectangle $[1,4]\\times[1,3]$ in the $uv$-plane.\n\n![](images/64/6423e8d59e07acda3c8d84e47852ad95f37d9fdf39c3669a8227c224a0cbb54a.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "1f2467b8-6aa5-598d-bd70-646d2f6c5588" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4410 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "81de6675-7ea1-553a-9697-f6798481631b" :step/math-academy-id 27105 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199253}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199253 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198362 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198236 :question/id #uuid "6d2cd5bf-899f-5b81-9512-ee98489d17ac" :question/math-academy-id "e-7322"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6d98056-016e-520b-b752-d6a529f2c02d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049373}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198949} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199005} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371450} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491121} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491131} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491167} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491193} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500369}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Rectangular Preimage Given a Transformation"} :step/id #uuid "a16a89f0-4b6c-571e-8667-09aa727ed945" :step/math-academy-id 27106 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199276}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199254 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198363 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198237 :question/id #uuid "c78f087b-f0e5-58e6-a7a1-92a78c85c4d9" :question/math-academy-id "e-7323"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9156e571-2fe9-5347-95da-c1c97de1a3c9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050822} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050984}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199004} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199006} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371457} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371464} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371471} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373298} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491144} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491164} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491173} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500364} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500373}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Transformation That Maps a Region Onto a Type I Region"} :step/id #uuid "548a6670-5a09-5138-83d9-a4ddc9f4a9dc" :step/math-academy-id 27107} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199255 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199453 :tutorial/content "We often use transformations to map a region in the $xy$-plane to a type I region in the $uv$-plane.\n\nFor example, let $D$ be the region bounded by the curve $(x+y)^2 - y = 2$ and the line $y=1,$  as shown in the diagram below. \n\n![](images/d7/d7df2aa53d64cbd13c784a6dc5d827027f56cc8e63917bbd9a324f7576f3dfa6.png)\n\n \n\nThis is not a type I region because the lower curve is not an explicit function of $x.$ \n\nLet's map $D$ to a type I region in the $uv$-plane. First, let $\\mathbf T$ be the transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).\n$$\n\nThe equations of the bounding curves suggest the following for the transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{u}}={\\color{blue}{x+y}}, \\qquad {\\color{red}{v}}={\\color{red}{y}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\color{blue}{u}}={\\color{blue}{x+y}}$ and ${\\color{red}{v}}={\\color{red}{y}}$ into the equation of the lower curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+y)^{2}-y=2\\,⟹\\,{u}^{2}-v=2\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{2}-2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is the equation of an upward-opening parabola. \n\nSubstituting $y = v$ into the equation of the line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=1\\,⟹\\,v=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the parabola and line in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}v={u}^{2}-2 \\\\ v=1\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}u=\\pm \\sqrt{3} \\\\ v=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm \\sqrt3, 1).$\n\n![](images/5b/5b52258abe08675938210de54c53dafe474e753249341c36833b8286259144ee.png)\n\nFinally, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the following type I representation: \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}^{-1}(D)  = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: -\\sqrt3 \\leq u \\leq \\sqrt3, \\quad u^2-2 \\leq v \\leq 1 \\big\\}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6de07339-5bd1-5441-867a-164b51b62c5e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4411 :tutorial/title "Transformations That Map a Region to a Type I Region"} :step/id #uuid "708c40ea-21c0-5791-80ec-1f7934311a44" :step/math-academy-id 27113 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199254}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199276 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198376 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198018 :question/id #uuid "87c2cf96-8772-5b28-b6d8-a5616b38543f" :question/math-academy-id "e-12337"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de3fa2fa-304c-583a-8c73-a5ff1db972e2" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198556} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198582} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371920} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371934} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398500} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491130} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491169} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491212} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500307} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500362}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Transformation That Maps a Rectangular Region Onto a Given One"} :step/id #uuid "b3d2c730-410c-5244-b2a9-29f37c6ec44b" :step/math-academy-id 44206 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199255}}] :activity/title "Transformations of Regions Between Curves" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198480}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500407} :activity/id #uuid "5236f00e-3e0c-590b-ab92-46b5776952a8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049032 :topic/id #uuid "d4ff589f-1af0-5477-b78d-91f6dfb7f322" :topic/math-academy-id 4134} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500388 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500307 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38837c3f-3a25-53aa-af88-6095dbd44aa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡v}{u+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499769} {:answer/id #uuid "92967e3c-5864-5abd-aa87-5d7fcf6ac098" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{u\\operatorname{ln}⁡v}{u+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499940} {:answer/id #uuid "d04f42a5-067b-5fa1-9332-07ed3d5b6ff9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡u}{v+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500083} {:answer/id #uuid "de6f5dca-392e-5e63-929b-f3df98856bee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{u\\operatorname{ln}⁡v}{v+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500113} {:answer/id #uuid "dff1ee65-ce03-521b-908a-5cef080fba1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡v}{uv+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500117}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499940} :answer-field/id #uuid "c414baf9-635b-5e55-85c0-f6df87c6300c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500260}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4c4c2566-13c3-50ff-974c-e99594494c43" :question/math-academy-id "q-161087" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $D$ enclosed in the first quadrant of the $xy$-plane between the lines and curves \n\n$$\nx=3y,\\,x=7y,\\,{e}^{x}=\\frac{2}{{e}^{y}},\\,{e}^{x}=\\frac{6}{{e}^{y}}.\n$$\n\nA transformation $T,$ where\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nmaps a rectangular region $Δ$ with sides parallel to the $u$- and $v$-axes from the $uv$-plane onto $D$ in the $xy$-plane. Given that\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (\\,\\bbox[5pt,white,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,,\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡v}{u+1}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhat could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "We would like to find a rectangular region of the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,a\\le u\\le b,\\,\\,c\\le v\\le d\\} \\\\  & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,a\\le u(x,y)\\le b,\\,\\,c\\le v(x,y)\\le d\\},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the equations of the left, right, bottom, and top sides are\n\n$$\nu(x,y)=a,\\,u(x,y)=b,\\,v(x,y)=c,\\,v(x,y)=d.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze the boundary of our region $D.$ \n\nRewriting the equations \n\n$$\nx=3y,\\,x=7y,\\,{e}^{x}=\\frac{2}{{e}^{y}},\\,{e}^{x}=\\frac{6}{{e}^{y}}\n$$\n\nby moving both variables $x$ and $y$ to the left-hand side, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{y}=3,\\,\\frac{x}{y}=7,\\,{e}^{x+y}=2,\\,{e}^{x+y}=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we put $u(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y}$ and $v(x,y)={e}^{x+y},$ we can define the transformation ${T}^{-1}$ as\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:(x,y)\\to (\\,\\frac{x}{y}\\,,\\,{e}^{x+y}).\n$$\n\nTo find $T,$ we should express $x$ and $y$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$ Recall that we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}u=\\frac{x}{y} \\\\ v={e}^{x+y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, $x=uy.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get \n\n$$\nv={e}^{uy+y}\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡v=uy+y\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡v}{u+1}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡v}{u+1}$ back into $x=uy,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{u\\operatorname{ln}⁡v}{u+1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the transformation $T$ can be given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (\\,\\frac{u\\operatorname{ln}⁡v}{u+1}\\,,\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡v}{u+1}).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0c70884a-daa4-52bf-a53a-39f975c92f7e"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500390 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500369 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05ce61c2-01b8-5043-9f9a-753fdddeddad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-4\\le u\\le 2,\\,-2\\le v\\le 5\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499663} {:answer/id #uuid "07e38fd3-f652-5a5c-a58c-99eca697a72e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 3,\\,-3\\le v\\le 7\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499668} {:answer/id #uuid "08c4e03b-acc3-5d4c-aeca-ac6dd9e5dad3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-5\\le u\\le 3,\\,-3\\le v\\le 1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499671} {:answer/id #uuid "d71c5929-deb6-587c-ad08-7219b0ced4ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,5\\le u\\le 8,\\,1\\le v\\le 9\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500094} {:answer/id #uuid "f65c68cb-d554-5294-95f5-3459a5bd5fd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,3\\le u\\le 7,\\,-5\\le v\\le 3\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500162}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499671} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b105983-4e5e-54ad-b400-c47eac3c85af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500214}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e3b9d256-a56c-59c5-89ce-db8a163e82f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-85304" :question/problem "Consider the region $D,$ given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,2y-5\\le x\\le 3+2y,\\,0\\le y\\le 4\\}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere $u=x-2y$ and $v=y-3$ Find the representation of ${T}^{-1}(D)$ in the $uv$-plane." :question/worked-solution "Notice that ${T}^{-1}(D)$ is the image of $D$ under the action of the transformation ${T}^{-1}.$  \n\nLet's find the image of ${T}^{-1}(D)$ in the $uv$-plane. To do that, we write the region $D$ in terms of $u$ and $v,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(D) & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,2y-5\\le x\\le 3+2y,\\,0\\le y\\le 4\\} \\\\  & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,(2y-5)-2y\\le x-2y\\le (3+2y)-2y,\\,0-3\\le y-3\\le 4-3\\} \\\\  & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-5\\le u\\le 3,\\,-3\\le v\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "148c81a9-4b53-5838-b141-ec4a2153b3c7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500422}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500407 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500364 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4209f830-c9a8-5754-bc99-a5cd64e9d1e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,5-{u}^{2}\\le v\\le 4\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499778} {:answer/id #uuid "8c14e9d1-1ebc-5911-83b4-6684991c4a5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,4\\le v\\le 5-{u}^{2}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499928} {:answer/id #uuid "a1308d93-95f0-5ab2-a9e7-6b7e0551f91d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,5\\le v\\le 5-{u}^{2}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499966} {:answer/id #uuid "b29aa89a-331b-5b26-81e9-f1db6a760e94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 2,\\,5-{u}^{2}\\le v\\le 4\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500011} {:answer/id #uuid "f4095012-39a4-5bc8-a2ee-c30c5669c7bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 2,\\,4\\le v\\le 5-{u}^{2}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500157}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499928} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ec5b769-0f6c-59ac-907b-1613a4af2250" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500186}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d78c4c29-1d34-52bf-9160-d17820674c44" :question/math-academy-id "q-83640" :question/problem "Let $D$ be the finite region bounded by the curve \n${(x+3y)}^{2}+y=5$ and the line $y=4.$ Consider the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere $u=x+3y$ and $v=y.$ Find the representation of ${T}^{-1}(D)$ in the $uv$-plane." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we are given that \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x+3y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x+3y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x+3y)}^{2}+y=5\\,⟹\\,{u}^{2}+v=5\\,⟹\\,v=5-{u}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=4\\,⟹\\,v=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}v=5-{u}^{2} \\\\ v=4\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}u=\\pm 1 \\\\ v=4.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 1,4).$\n\n![](images/94/9427985b45271bec39577b35334311c11b71983d1cc5f8b9ac35c6fe88f29d98.png)\n\nAs a result, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}(D)=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,4\\le v\\le 5-{u}^{2}\\}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5c991136-4174-5b8c-a072-9dbe197bf649" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500390}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500411 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500362 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e80adab-46fc-5b59-80ee-fdeba6442a4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499839} {:answer/id #uuid "80190feb-7e03-5fd4-a6e1-0f0193ad096c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499897} {:answer/id #uuid "b0927577-5c45-58ef-8b51-30b332e1da81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{y}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500008} {:answer/id #uuid "cd72cf12-ab3a-53fc-97d2-6cd9ca4f5545" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500073} {:answer/id #uuid "f4170e9d-5757-5fcd-9424-ce06df4b08fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500158}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500008} :answer-field/id #uuid "b86da554-3d18-5e0e-b7f0-853ee5710407" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500256}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d284dc90-b074-5607-a72d-8d0fe39284a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-161081" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $D$ enclosed in the first quadrant of the $xy$-plane between the curves\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}},\\,y=\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}},\\,y=3x,\\,y=5x.\n$$\n\nA transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nmaps a rectangular region $Δ$ with sides parallel to the $u$- and $v$-axes from the $uv$-plane onto $D$ in the $xy$-plane. Given that\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:(x,y)\\to ({x}^{2}y\\,,\\,\\bbox[5pt,white,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhat could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "We would like to find a rectangular region of the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,a\\le u\\le b,\\,\\,c\\le v\\le d\\} \\\\  & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,a\\le u(x,y)\\le b,\\,\\,c\\le v(x,y)\\le d\\},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the equations of the left, right, bottom, and top sides are\n\n$$\nu(x,y)=a,\\,u(x,y)=b,\\,v(x,y)=c,\\,v(x,y)=d.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze the boundary of our region $D.$ \n\nRe-writing the equations \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}},\\,y=\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}},\\,y=3x,\\,y=5x\n$$\n\nby moving both variables $x$ and $y$ to the left-hand side, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y=2,\\,{x}^{2}y=4,\\,\\frac{y}{x}=3,\\,\\frac{y}{x}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we put $u(x,y)={x}^{2}y$ and $v(x,y)=\\frac{y}{x},$ we can define the transformation ${T}^{-1}$ as\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:(x,y)\\to (\\,{x}^{2}y\\,,\\,\\frac{y}{x}).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6df31d5a-d97b-5683-b5dd-1ec918e08ea4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500388}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500422 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500373 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37662fea-e009-5c9a-9490-fc42a75cf611" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-\\sqrt{3}\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,0\\le v\\le 3-{u}^{2}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499768} {:answer/id #uuid "4f24bf7e-5972-5a21-8362-2837be9b7902" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-\\sqrt{3}\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,-1\\le v\\le 3-{u}^{2}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499805} {:answer/id #uuid "6e9a4290-f95e-517a-87d3-3d343a75caa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-3\\le u\\le 3,\\,0\\le v\\le 2-{u}^{2}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499862} {:answer/id #uuid "c745a9ed-7b4d-5886-950b-04aa803596d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,0\\le v\\le 2-{u}^{2}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500059} {:answer/id #uuid "f83be70d-f8ea-59a1-9672-0ba130685944" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,-\\sqrt{3}\\le v\\le 3-{u}^{2}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500165}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499768} :answer-field/id #uuid "13d9fa7c-95dc-5280-829e-a7b5d17f12e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500189}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "eabf1331-ca5b-594d-af21-53db9e3ebf21" :question/math-academy-id "q-85307" :question/problem "Let $D$ be the finite region bounded by the curve \n$(2x-3y)^{2}+y=3$ and the line $y=0.$ Consider the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere $u=2x-3y$ and $v=y.$ Find the representation of ${T}^{-1}(D)$ in the $uv$-plane." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we are given that \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2x-3y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSubstituting $2x-3y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x-3y)^{2}+y=3\\,⟹\\,{u}^{2}+v=3\\,⟹\\,v=3-{u}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=0\\,⟹\\,v=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}v=3-{u}^{2} \\\\ v=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}u=\\pm \\sqrt{3} \\\\ v=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm \\sqrt{3},0).$\n\n![](images/17/172daaae3de5212c5e182965b66bf2afcade59e2d5eb156c7dbff214e3bfb040.png)\n\nAs a result, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}(D)=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-\\sqrt{3}\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,0\\le v\\le 3-{u}^{2}\\}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8c534e8b-12d5-5861-8fad-aaddc6760864" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500411}}] :activity/title "Transformations of Regions Between Curves" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499646}]}
